This tutorial series takes one real field survey from paper notes to a Darwin
Core Archive. Along the way, you will learn NAHPU's core workflow—projects,
personnel, taxa, sites, collecting events, specimen records, narratives, and
export—and see how a structured digital catalog keeps data linked and consistent
in ways paper forms cannot.

The data come from a young Heru Handika (let's call it that way, although he is not that old yet). 
Now, he is the NAHPU project lead and the writer of these tutorials. The
tutorials refer to him in the third person so that anyone can teach this
material as written. Feel free to
[contact him if you have questions](https://hhandika.com) about the tutorials
or NAHPU itself.

In September 2014, he surveyed small mammals on Mt. Tujuh (Gunung Tujuh) in
Kerinci, Jambi, Indonesia, keeping two paper records: printed specimen forms and
a handwritten field journal. That same year he failed two standardized English
tests, and his journal shows it. In
[Basic tutorial 6](/en/tutorials/basic/narrative/), you will also clean up the
English before entering it.

{% aside type="note" title="Names in these tutorials" %}
Among the people you enter into NAHPU, Heru Handika is the only real person
named. Teammates, the local guide, and the boatman appear under fictional names:
Bima Sampel, Citra Contoh, Dian Teladan, Eko Pemandu, and Fajar Nelayan.
{% /aside %}

{% aside type="note" title="Acknowledgments" %}
[Andalas University](https://www.unand.ac.id/) (Universitas Andalas), the
